um one of the  funniest little details in this entire  saga of people protesting at the homes  of not only supreme court justices but  also republican senators is that  republican senator susan collins from  maine  decided to call the police because there  was chalk writing on the sidewalk  outside of her house that this is like  the epitome  of the triggered snowflake and i i  i am sensitive to  others being sensitive about protests at  their homes and we had a very good  conversation on the show yesterday with  coleman hughes about it  but the police over chalk  when you supposedly are a defender of  speech you know the the free the i'm  fine having a good faith conversation  about protests at people's houses i'm  glad to do it  but when you're talking about  free speech absolutists who all of a  sudden say chalk isn't okay that's a bit  of a problem business insider reports  susan collins called the police to refor  report quote defacement of public  property after someone wrote an abortion  rights message in chalk on a public  sidewalk outside of her house  republican senator susan collins of  maine one of just two nominally  pro-choice republicans in the senate  called the police saturday night because  of an abortion rights message written in  chalk in front of her house in bangor  maine  according to the banger daily news  officers responded to a call at 9 20  because a message appeared on the  sidewalk it read susie please mainers  want whpa vote yes clean up your mess  whpa means the women's health protection  bill which would codify abortion rights  afforded by roe v wade into federal law  this is an idea that is an important  idea it's being increasingly discussed  well okay the supreme court can overturn  roe v wade by legal precedent but if we  codify  abortion rights into federal law  it overrides and supersedes that  the message was not overtly threatening  said wade betters a spokesperson for the  police department we are grateful  that susan collins said to the daily  news we are grateful to the banger  police officers and the city public  works employees who responded to the  defacement of public property in front  of our home  guys i mean we're not talking about  poster paint here we're not talking  about spray paint we're talking about  chalk which washes away when it rains  or with a bucket or a hose to call this  defacement this isn't graffiti this is  what kids do all the time they write  with chalk on the sidewalk and um  there's this  i don't want to put all of this on susan  collins because it's not it's not  uniquely her that's the hypocrite here  but it's the big picture of all of this  on the one hand republicans have been so  hesitant to say  the president led and incited an  insurrection and there should be no  consequences on the one hand  and then on the other hand someone wrote  with chalk call the cops  it it's it's one of these uh the the  message here  is a message of authoritarianism  of shutting down public speech and  protest  throwing things in a senator at a  senator of course not absolutely not  going on the property of individuals to  prote absolutely not  um you know what whatever else  no writing with chalk on the sidewalk  that seems like a real problem uh uh  when that now becomes something that  police are called over you can't write  with chalk on a sidewalk  but then you can tell the woman to carry  a pregnancy to term or you don't want to  hold trump accountable for the things  that he did so i think that this is an  outrageous  little micro episode about how the big  picture conversation is kind of missing  the forest for the trees and and by the  way i got a few angry emails after my  conversation yesterday with coleman  hughes from people saying david how can  you be  worried about protests at someone's  house  when women are being disenfranchised  from even being able to make decisions  about their own bodies decisions which  can be life and death they're two  separate situations  i am  you completely overwhelmingly  unequivocally disgusted by what we  believe to be the forthcoming decision  on roe v wade  and i also think there's a good faith  discussion to be had  about protesting at the houses of  supreme court justices these it's okay  to be able to discuss the two things  separately and a handful of people or so  maybe more like a dozen  didn't like that we were even having the  discussion and by the way i also have a  problem with that i know about this  whole we're just asking questions thing  and how it can be used to cover  uh in ways that aren't particularly  productive oh when when tucker says  some people say  ten thousand people died from the covet  vaccine we're just asking questions on  the show i i get the the problem  problematic nature of just asking  questions  but to say i shouldn't even be having  the conversation about protest strategy  and tactics  no guys that that's not uh  that that's not any kind of support of  robust discussion  that i can agree with so maybe rethink  that we will have more on all of these  stories on the david pakman show  instagram which you can find at david  pakman show and if you want to see me  with a big black and white animal was it  a cow or was it a zebra many people are  asking what exactly was the animal i was  pictured with  follow me on my personal instagram at  david.pacman we'll take a quick break  and be right back  one of our sponsors is called privacy  privacy is a totally free service that  i've been using for years privacy lets  me create virtual payment cards with one  click and when i use the virtual card  the money is taken out of my bank  account you can create multiple cards  delete them freeze them set a spending  limit on them let's say i'm signing up  for a free trial and it requires a  credit card privacy will autofill a  virtual card number and then after  signing up i can delete that virtual  card so i know i'm not going to be  charged again something really useful  and very practical or let's say i'm  ordering food over the phone but i left  my wallet somewhere i don't have my real  credit card on me i can use a virtual  card on my privacy mobile app but really  the best part is  you're not out there exposing your real  credit card number so you're not as  susceptible to data breaches and  identity theft privacy is a totally free  service and you'll get five bucks to  spend when you go to privacy.com  pacman the link is right underneath this  video

